\f1\fs22 Kathy insisted on sleeping in the same room as Sarah. \'91Doctor\'92s orders,\'92 she explained.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley took the equally well-appointed suite across the hall, and ruminated on how they\'92d escape. If Sarah wanted to stay, and Kathy wanted to play doctor, well and good. But Hayley had no intention of playing the third wheel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slept in fits and starts, dreams and nightmares until, from a ever-looping scene in which she was marrying a man dressed in a burger clown\'92s outfit and make-up. She knew the bridegroom must be Alexander, but the more she tried to study his face, the more his features morphed and faded. She woke with a thumping heart, and sat up as straight as a flagpole. Threw her legs over the side of the bed and tried to calm her chest with slow even breaths. She cast her gaze around the room, big as a lounge room, and could make out thin grey outlines of wall, ceiling and furniture, as if the room was moonlit. Strange, she thought. She was sure the aspect of the room faced southerly. As her eyes adjusted, she saw Kathy asleep in a couch by the window. She stood up cautiously\'97her head was still light and swimming\'97and went to the ensuite for water.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander!\'92 she said to the reflection in the mirror. \'91How can I marry Alexander?\'92 And if she didn\'92t marry him, didn\'92t take Eloise\'92s Faustian bargain, what would become of her? Sarah doubted even her exemplary connections in the elite Airborne community could defend her against Eloise Shakti. \'91You can\'92t marry him. You can\'92t marry 
\f2\i anyone,
\f1\i0 \'92 she told the reflection. A teared formed at the corner of the mirror\'92s eye, and ran a glistening runnel down its shiny cheek. \'91Tony, I got a taxing pox. And now, so do you.\'92 The mirror sobbed and Sarah was glad she could project her anguish into it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah splashed water over her face; she filled a glass and drained it with thirsty gulps; replenished it and took it to a chair opposite the sleeping Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She stared at the fireplace with unfocused eyes, rolled the glass around in her hand. She couldn\'92t marry Alexander, could she? As reprehensible as the Eloise\'92s propositions were, the upsides titillated deeply entrained appetites her Airborne lineage engendered. E\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 very Airborne dreamt of ascending above the Layer farther and farther above the Layer. Below the Layer was resource-limited, finite. There was no where to go but into the dirt. Above the Layer was limitless, infinite. Airborne sensibilities valued ascent as always, no matter the means. In the eyes of society (which, by definition, excluded the Cloudborne), the virtue of the climb accrues to the climber. There were some Airborne who spread the belief they are prototypical angels. They claimed they could see the halos. An Airborne with a halo was truly virtuous indeed.\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah reflected on the power and influence of her proposed benefactor. Sarah could live a life someone of even her relatively high station could only dream of. And her son \'85 he could inherit, all this?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yet a pit remained in her stomach, a deep cave into which her heart plummeted, along with her sense of happiness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When was she ever happy?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Challenging the status quo. Offending her friends with her fraternising ways, consorting with the Elvi, the underclass of the underclass. Clones, not human. Just engineered servants, objects to be ordered about and humiliated while ascribing their obvious talents to the technology of their cloning. The Elvi were empty vessels without the genius of human engineering filling them to the brim.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, deep down, always knew that was taxing bull tax. The Elvi were people too. She knew that, even as she \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 played the obnoxious tourist in so many Elvi bars, exploiting the Elvi as a means of making her friends uncomfortable, squeamish, even fearing for their lives. Sarah revelled in dragging them through Cloudborne squalor, confident that nothing could truly harm her. When the Elvi fought\'97with fists and pool cues and nunchuks and bathroom sinks\'97she was exhilarated. She was never so happy. She loved the circus and so she acted out, giving reins to the daredevil in her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sure, the Elvi diet\'97coupled with the genetic roulette the house dealt them\'97should knock anyone\'92s lifespan down to about four decades, but there\'92s only so much fun and laughter one can extract from shaming their lifestyle. Besides, many cultures and civilisations in the course of history ate worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But no other culture in the history of the world sang better. Sang 
\f2\i differently
\f1\i0 , perhaps, but not better. And now, with their Great Teacher, they were putting aside their lyric-fuelled violence (they all sounded absurd to Sarah, even the words of the Great Teacher) and they were 
\f2\i succeeding
\f1\i0 . One of five of all plays on music streaming services in Ulyssia were Elvi songs. As Elvi all over the world began recording the new songs, it seemed the Elvi would become an unstoppable international phenomenon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All because of the Great Teacher. Tony. Mr Fairy Boxers. The thought of Tony eked an unlikely smile from Sarah\'92s lips, until the nagging question ambushed her, as it often did when she though of Tony. Is her baby his? Since the news of her pregnancy, Tony was singularly fawning and attentive, perhaps betraying his stake in the pregnancy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Immediately, Sarah called herself a cow for thinking such things. But \'85 if only she could be sure. Even in the face of Kathy\'92s evidence that her baby is carrying the DNA of a long-dead scientist, Sarah was skeptical. On the other hand, what was the chance a drunk man thought it\'92d be OK to touch up a passed-out woman? A likelihood to a certainty, Sarah thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yet it was Tony that the hypothetical court in her head was accusing. Mr Fairy Boxers. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She shuddered. As loathsome as she found Alexander, she could never be with Tony either. Could she? Why couldn\'92t she find someone among her Airborne, who has all the qualities she likes about Tony? They can\'92t all be dicks, can they? Perhaps belief in halos and angels made it so.
